This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   - (Established in a prior segment, still governing this one) User ran `git push`, asked to "PR it" (create GitHub PR for `datum/epic-287` against `main`), reported "lots of merge conflicts", explicitly chose **"Merge main into the branch, resolve once"** (one merge commit, not rebase), then upon discovering 7 files where main shipped real independent bug fixes to App-side files that epic-287 had deleted, explicitly chose **"Forward-port now, in this merge"** — manually re-apply each of main's fixes onto the CrossPlatform-Foundation (unified) equivalents, with TDD where it touches behavior.
   - User asked "we dropped DuckDB for SQLite didnt we" (answered/verified in an earlier segment).
   - User said **"yes continue and update all project memory about sqlite"** — the "continue" clause is what has driven all autonomous work in this segment and the one before it: resuming the Domain-protocol-first forward-port work, then cascading test-fake fixes, then merge-conflict resolution.
   - No new user messages occurred during this segment — all work has been autonomous continuation per "Auto Mode Active" system guidance (bias toward acting without stopping to ask, though the guidance also says to stop when genuinely blocked on a decision only the user can make — which is relevant to the ci.yml conflict currently in progress).

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Epic 287 "fork unification": Business/UseCases source lives once in CPF (`Record-CrossPlatform-Foundation/Sources/Business/UseCases/*.swift`) but is compiled twice against two independently-versioned `Domain` module forks — root `Package.swift`'s `Business`/`Presentation` targets point their `path:` at the CPF source directory but declare their `Domain`/`Logging` dependency as `.product(name: "Domain", package: "Record-Foundation")` (i.e., Record-Foundation's Domain, for macOS/App), while `Record-CrossPlatform-Foundation/Package.swift` compiles the same sources against its own local Domain (for cross-platform/Windows).
   - `swift build --package-path Record-CrossPlatform-Foundation --build-tests` used throughout as the sole ground-truth compile check; SourceKit's live diagnostics have repeatedly proven stale/lagging this entire session and must not be trusted over an actual build run.
   - Protocol conformance ripple effect: two Domain protocol edits (`getRecordingForRecovery`, `clearNeedsRetranscription`, `markSessionStarted(audioURL:)`) made in an earlier segment broke every test fake/stub conforming to `RecordingReadRepository`/`RecordingWriteRepository`/`TranscriptWriteRepository` across the whole test suite — a wide mechanical fix-out pass, now complete for the CPF package.
   - `git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U` / `git status --short <paths>` used to get precise conflict status codes (`UU` = both modified, `DU` = deleted by us, `UA` = added by them/us — the deleted-by-us files here are actually the ones **we** intentionally want removed since main independently fixed files epic-287 had already deleted).
   - `justfile`/`Justfile` (identical content) at repo root: a `just`-based task runner with `build`, `test`, `test-linux` (Docker), `lint` recipes — confirmed to exist both locally and on `origin/main`, validating that main's CI self-hosted-runner approach (`just test`) is real, established infra and not a stray/broken edit.
   - CI/CD architecture split under active conflict: HEAD's epic-287-specific `drift-tripwire` job (guards remaining Domain/Logging fork pairs, added in this branch's own recent commits `3e947c1a`/`eb1bb7df`) vs. origin/main's self-hosted-runner (`mancini`, macOS ARM64) + LFS + `just test` approach (added because GitHub-hosted macOS minutes bill 10x and break LFS-backed `Prebuilts/*` package resolution) — these are NOT simply alternative equivalent implementations; each side carries real, distinct, load-bearing information that must not be silently dropped.
   - `PostToolUse:Edit` hook `banned-patterns.sh` — blocks edits (or flags post-edit state) when merge-conflict markers (`<<<<<<<`, `=======`, `>>>>>>>`) are detected in a file; fired transiently mid-sequence while other conflict blocks in the same file were still unresolved, resolved once all blocks in the file were cleared (verified via `grep -n "^<<<<<<<\|^=======\|^>>>>>>>"` returning exit code 1).

